/** Default SAT & Kid Question Bank **/
window.DEFAULT_QUESTIONS = [{"id":"sat_rw_01","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"Information and Ideas","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"In the essay \"On Liberty,\" John Stuart Mill argues that \"the peculiar evil of silencing the expression of an opinion is, that it is robbing the human race; posterity as well as the existing generation; those who dissent from the opinion, still more than those who hold it.\"","question":"What is Mill's main point in this passage from \"On Liberty\"?","choices":{"A":"Suppressing opinions robs future generations of the chance to hear them, even if they disagree with them.","B":"It is harmful to silence opinions that are held by a majority of people.","C":"People who dissent from an opinion are more likely to be harmed by its suppression than those who hold it.","D":"It is important to respect all opinions, even if they are wrong."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Mill argues that suppressing opinions is a harm to everyone, including future generations, because it prevents them from hearing and potentially engaging with these ideas."}},{"id":"sat_rw_02","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"Standard English Conventions","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"The researchers concluded that, in fact, as many as half of the hunters in the groups they studied were female. This finding was a direct challenge to the accepted view that female participation in hunting was negligible.","question":"Which choice best combines the sentences to create a grammatically correct and stylistically effective sentence?","choices":{"A":"This finding was a direct challenge to the accepted view, that female participation in hunting was negligible.","B":"This finding directly challenged the accepted view, that female participation in hunting was negligible.","C":"This finding directly challenged the accepted view that female participation in hunting was negligible.","D":"This finding, a direct challenge to the accepted view, that female participation in hunting was negligible."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Choice C keeps a correct grammatical structure and avoids an unnecessary comma before the restrictive 'that' clause."}},{"id":"sat_rw_03","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"Expression of Ideas","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"The article argues that the way people use technology can be divided into two categories. One category is the use of technology for specific tasks, which the authors call \"instrumental\" use. The other category is the use of technology for pleasure or entertainment, which the authors call \"noninstrumental\" use. This distinction is important because it can help explain why people use technology in different ways, and how these different uses can affect people’s lives.","question":"What is the main idea of this article?","choices":{"A":"Instrumental use of technology is more important than noninstrumental use.","B":"The distinction between instrumental and noninstrumental use of technology is important for understanding how people use technology in the twenty-first century.","C":"Noninstrumental use of technology is more important than instrumental use.","D":"The use of technology has a significant impact on people’s lives in the twenty-first century."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The passage centers on why distinguishing between instrumental and noninstrumental technology use helps explain different usage patterns and effects."}},{"id":"sat_rw_04","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"Information and Ideas","difficulty":"Hard","question":{"paragraph":"The author is most interested in the impact of the internet on the way information is spread, focusing on the ways that individuals have become more powerful in shaping and distributing information. One of the key points the author makes is that information is no longer solely controlled by institutions and governments. This can be seen in the rise of citizen journalism. The author also notes the downsides of the internet, such as the spread of misinformation. However, the author’s primary focus is on the positive ways that the internet has empowered individuals.","question":"Which statement best describes the author’s main point?","choices":{"A":"The internet has revolutionized the way information is distributed, making it more accessible and empowering individuals.","B":"The internet has both positive and negative effects on the way information is shared.","C":"Traditional sources of information are no longer as trustworthy as they once were.","D":"The internet has made the world a smaller place, making it easier for people to connect with one another."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"While acknowledging downsides, the author's primary focus is on the positive ways the internet has empowered individuals."}},{"id":"sat_rw_05","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"Craft and Structure","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"The novel \"Jane Eyre\" by Charlotte Bronte features scenes that use several literary devices, such as foreshadowing and imagery, to create a powerful and engaging reading experience. The novel is narrated by Jane Eyre herself, and the reader only sees the world through Jane’s eyes. This literary device is key in building empathy for Jane and her character.","question":"In this passage, the use of the first-person perspective primarily serves to","choices":{"A":"create suspense and intrigue for the reader.","B":"develop the reader’s understanding of the story’s setting.","C":"emphasize the importance of the speaker’s feelings and experiences.","D":"suggest that the speaker is addressing a large audience."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"First-person narration lets the reader experience Jane's feelings directly, building empathy."}},{"id":"sat_math_01","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Algebra","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"A research drone ascends at a constant rate of 14 meters per second from an initial platform 28 meters above ground level. The height h(t), in meters, after t seconds is modeled by h(t) = 14t + 28.","question":"After how many seconds will the drone reach an altitude of 140 meters?","choices":{"A":"6 seconds","B":"8 seconds","C":"10 seconds","D":"12 seconds"},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"14t + 28 = 140 → 14t = 112 → t = 8 seconds."}},{"id":"sat_math_adv_01","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Advanced Math & Data","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"A ball is launched vertically upward from a platform. Its height h(t), in meters above the ground, after t seconds is given by the quadratic function h(t) = -5(t - 3)² + 52.","question":"What is the maximum height, in meters, that the ball reaches above the ground?","choices":{"A":"3 meters","B":"45 meters","C":"47 meters","D":"52 meters"},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"In vertex form h(t) = a(t - h)² + k with a = -5 < 0, the vertex (3, 52) gives the maximum height of 52 meters at t = 3 seconds."}},{"id":"sat_math_02","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Geometry & Trig","difficulty":"Hard","question":{"paragraph":"A right circular cylinder has a volume of 45π cubic centimeters and a radius of 3 centimeters. The formula for the volume of a cylinder is V = πr²h.","question":"What is the total surface area (including both circular bases) of this cylinder?","choices":{"A":"30π cm²","B":"39π cm²","C":"48π cm²","D":"54π cm²"},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"45π = 9πh → h = 5. Surface area = 2πr² + 2πrh = 18π + 30π = 48π cm²."}},{"id":"sci_stem_01","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"Science & Biology","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"CRISPR-Cas9 is a genome-editing tool that acts like molecular scissors, allowing geneticists to cut DNA at precise locations directed by a guide RNA sequence.","question":"What is the primary role of the guide RNA (gRNA) in the CRISPR-Cas9 complex?","choices":{"A":"It provides the metabolic energy required to cleave the cell membrane.","B":"It binds to the matching target DNA sequence so the Cas9 enzyme cuts at the exact intended locus.","C":"It synthesizes new amino acids directly inside the ribosome.","D":"It prevents the cell from undergoing mitosis."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The guide RNA's complementary sequence binds the target DNA, directing Cas9 to cut at the right spot."}},{"id":"kid_spell_01","targetProfile":"kid","domain":"6+ Spelling Bee","difficulty":"Easy","question":{"paragraph":"🐝 Phonics Clue: It starts with 'FR' and ends with 'END'. It means someone you love to play and share with!","question":"Which is the correct spelling for the word 'FRIEND'?","choices":{"A":"FREND","B":"FRIEND","C":"FREIND","D":"FRAND"},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Remember the fun rhyme: 'A FRIEND is a pal to the END!' -> F - R - I - E - N - D."}},{"id":"kid_comp_01","targetProfile":"kid","domain":"6+ Story Comprehension","difficulty":"Easy","question":{"paragraph":"🦁 Leo the Little Lion loved wearing his bright yellow sun hat. One windy afternoon, a big gust of wind blew his hat right into the tall green grass! Leo's friend, Zara the tall Giraffe, looked down from above the trees and spotted the yellow hat right away.","question":"How was Zara the Giraffe able to find Leo's lost hat so quickly?","choices":{"A":"She used a pair of binoculars.","B":"She was tall enough to look down from above the trees and grass.","C":"She dug a hole under the river.","D":"It was glowing in the dark at midnight."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The story says Zara the tall Giraffe looked down from above the trees and spotted the yellow hat in the tall grass!"}},{"id":"kid_gram_01","targetProfile":"kid","domain":"6+ Grammar & Sentences","difficulty":"Easy","question":{"paragraph":"🏃 Grammar Tip: An ACTION VERB is a word that shows what someone or something DOES (like run, jump, read, or sing).","question":"Which word in this sentence is the ACTION VERB?\n'The happy puppy jumped over the red ball.'","choices":{"A":"puppy","B":"happy","C":"jumped","D":"ball"},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"'Jumped' is the action verb because it tells what the puppy did!"}},{"id":"sat_math_03","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Algebra","difficulty":"Easy","question":{"paragraph":"A parking garage charges a $4 entry fee plus $3 for each hour a car is parked. A driver paid $19 in total when leaving the garage.","question":"For how many hours was the car parked?","choices":{"A":"3 hours","B":"4 hours","C":"5 hours","D":"6 hours"},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Let h be the number of hours. 4 + 3h = 19, so 3h = 15 and h = 5 hours."}},{"id":"sat_math_04","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Algebra","difficulty":"Easy","question":{"paragraph":"A line in the xy-plane passes through the points (0, 2) and (3, 11).","question":"What is the slope of the line?","choices":{"A":"3","B":"4","C":"6","D":"9"},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Slope = (11 - 2) / (3 - 0) = 9 / 3 = 3."}},{"id":"sat_math_05","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Algebra","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"Two numbers have a sum of 10. The same two numbers have a difference of 4, where x is the greater number and y is the smaller number, so x + y = 10 and x - y = 4.","question":"What is the value of x?","choices":{"A":"6","B":"7","C":"8","D":"9"},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Adding the equations gives 2x = 14, so x = 7."}},{"id":"sat_math_06","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Algebra","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"A student is solving the inequality 2x - 5 > 9 for x.","question":"Which inequality is equivalent to the given inequality?","choices":{"A":"x < 2","B":"x > 2","C":"x < 7","D":"x > 7"},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Add 5 to both sides: 2x > 14. Divide by 2: x > 7."}},{"id":"sat_math_07","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Algebra","difficulty":"Easy","question":{"paragraph":"A function f is defined by f(x) = 2x + 3 for all real numbers x.","question":"What is the value of f(5)?","choices":{"A":"11","B":"13","C":"15","D":"17"},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"f(5) = 2(5) + 3 = 10 + 3 = 13."}},{"id":"sat_math_08","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Algebra","difficulty":"Hard","question":{"paragraph":"The expression (x + 3)² can be rewritten in the form x² + ax + 9, where a is a constant and the two expressions are equivalent for all values of x.","question":"What is the value of a?","choices":{"A":"3","B":"5","C":"6","D":"12"},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"(x + 3)² = x² + 6x + 9, so a = 6."}},{"id":"sat_math_09","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Algebra","difficulty":"Hard","question":{"paragraph":"Line p in the xy-plane has a slope of -2/3. Line q is perpendicular to line p.","question":"What is the slope of line q?","choices":{"A":"3/2","B":"2/3","C":"-2/3","D":"-3/2"},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Perpendicular lines have slopes whose product is -1. The negative reciprocal of -2/3 is 3/2."}},{"id":"sat_math_10","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Advanced Math & Data","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"The equation x² - 6x + 8 = 0 has two real solutions.","question":"What is the greater of the two solutions?","choices":{"A":"1","B":"2","C":"3","D":"4"},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"x² - 6x + 8 = (x - 2)(x - 4) = 0, so x = 2 or x = 4. The greater solution is 4."}},{"id":"sat_math_11","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Advanced Math & Data","difficulty":"Easy","question":{"paragraph":"For a positive integer n, the expression 3⁴ · 3² is equivalent to 3ⁿ.","question":"What is the value of n?","choices":{"A":"5","B":"6","C":"8","D":"24"},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"When multiplying powers with the same base, add the exponents: 4 + 2 = 6, so n = 6."}},{"id":"sat_math_12","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Advanced Math & Data","difficulty":"Easy","question":{"paragraph":"A student earned quiz scores of 72, 85, 90, and 93. Each quiz is scored out of 100 points.","question":"What is the mean (average) of the student's four quiz scores?","choices":{"A":"85","B":"84","C":"86","D":"87"},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Mean = (72 + 85 + 90 + 93) / 4 = 340 / 4 = 85."}},{"id":"sat_math_13","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Advanced Math & Data","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"A bag contains only red and blue marbles. There are 4 red marbles and 6 blue marbles in the bag. One marble will be selected at random from the bag.","question":"What is the probability that the marble selected will be red?","choices":{"A":"1/5","B":"3/10","C":"2/5","D":"3/5"},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"There are 4 red marbles out of 10 marbles in total, so the probability is 4/10 = 2/5."}},{"id":"sat_math_14","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Advanced Math & Data","difficulty":"Hard","question":{"paragraph":"The quadratic equation x² + 4x + k = 0, where k is a constant, has no real solutions.","question":"Which condition must be true for k?","choices":{"A":"k < 2","B":"k = 4","C":"k < 4","D":"k > 4"},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"For no real solutions the discriminant must be negative: 4² - 4(1)(k) < 0, so 16 - 4k < 0 and k > 4."}},{"id":"sat_math_15","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Advanced Math & Data","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"A population of bacteria starts with 300 bacteria and doubles every 5 hours, continuing at the same rate.","question":"How many bacteria will there be after 15 hours?","choices":{"A":"2,400","B":"1,200","C":"900","D":"1,800"},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"15 hours is 3 doubling periods. 300 · 2³ = 300 · 8 = 2,400."}},{"id":"sat_math_16","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Advanced Math & Data","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"In a survey of 240 students, 35% of the students said that science is their favorite subject.","question":"How many of the students surveyed said that science is their favorite subject?","choices":{"A":"72","B":"84","C":"90","D":"96"},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"35% of 240 = 0.35 · 240 = 84 students."}},{"id":"sat_math_17","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Geometry & Trig","difficulty":"Easy","question":{"paragraph":"The measures of two angles of a triangle are 50 degrees and 60 degrees.","question":"What is the measure, in degrees, of the third angle of the triangle?","choices":{"A":"50","B":"60","C":"65","D":"70"},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"The angles of a triangle sum to 180 degrees: 180 - 50 - 60 = 70 degrees."}},{"id":"sat_math_18","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Geometry & Trig","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"A circle has a radius of 7 centimeters. The circumference of a circle is given by C = 2πr, where r is the radius.","question":"What is the circumference, in centimeters, of the circle?","choices":{"A":"7π","B":"21π","C":"14π","D":"49π"},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"C = 2π(7) = 14π centimeters."}},{"id":"sat_math_19","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Geometry & Trig","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"A right triangle has legs with lengths of 6 units and 8 units.","question":"What is the length, in units, of the hypotenuse of the triangle?","choices":{"A":"9","B":"10","C":"12","D":"14"},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"By the Pythagorean theorem, c² = 6² + 8² = 36 + 64 = 100, so c = 10 units."}},{"id":"sat_math_20","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Geometry & Trig","difficulty":"Hard","question":{"paragraph":"A right triangle has a hypotenuse of length 13. One leg has length 5, and the other leg has length 12. Angle A is the angle opposite the side of length 5, so the side adjacent to angle A has length 12.","question":"What is the value of cos(A)?","choices":{"A":"12/13","B":"5/13","C":"13/12","D":"5/12"},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Cosine = adjacent / hypotenuse = 12 / 13."}},{"id":"sat_math_21","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Geometry & Trig","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"A trapezoid has bases with lengths of 8 meters and 12 meters and a height of 5 meters. The area of a trapezoid is the average of the lengths of the bases multiplied by the height.","question":"What is the area, in square meters, of the trapezoid?","choices":{"A":"40","B":"45","C":"50","D":"60"},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Area = ((8 + 12) / 2) · 5 = 10 · 5 = 50 square meters."}},{"id":"sat_math_22","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Geometry & Trig","difficulty":"Hard","question":{"paragraph":"A sector of a circle has a central angle of 90 degrees. The radius of the circle is 6 units.","question":"What is the area, in square units, of the sector?","choices":{"A":"3π","B":"6π","C":"18π","D":"9π"},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"The sector is 90/360 = 1/4 of the circle. Its area is (1/4)π(6)² = (1/4)(36π) = 9π square units."}},{"id":"sat_rw_06","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"Information and Ideas","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"When a city in Arizona replaced some of its sun-baked parking lots with planted trees, researchers tracked what happened over five summers. The shaded areas recorded afternoon surface temperatures up to 20 degrees Fahrenheit lower than nearby unshaded lots, and local businesses reported that more customers chose to walk between shops instead of driving.","question":"According to the passage, one effect of planting the trees was that","choices":{"A":"shoppers stopped visiting the local businesses near the lots.","B":"more people walked between shops instead of driving.","C":"the city removed all of its parking lots within five years.","D":"summer temperatures rose in the shaded areas."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The passage states that businesses reported more customers chose to walk between shops instead of driving. The other choices contradict or go beyond the text."}},{"id":"sat_rw_07","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"Information and Ideas","difficulty":"Hard","question":{"paragraph":"Honeybee colonies pollinate many crops, but their role is often overstated in one respect: while bees visit flowers to collect nectar and pollen for themselves, the pollination of the plant is, from the bee's perspective, incidental. The plant, however, has evolved bright colors and sweet rewards precisely to exploit this behavior, turning the bee's self-interested trips into a delivery service for its pollen.","question":"Based on the passage, pollination is best described as","choices":{"A":"a deliberate service that bees perform in order to help plants reproduce.","B":"a behavior plants try to prevent because it wastes their nectar.","C":"a side effect of bees collecting food, which plants have evolved to take advantage of.","D":"an activity that benefits bees but never benefits plants."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The passage says pollination is 'incidental' from the bee's perspective while plants evolved rewards to 'exploit this behavior,' making it a side effect plants take advantage of."}},{"id":"sat_rw_08","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"Information and Ideas","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"A public library's summer reading program was redesigned in 2023. Instead of requiring children to read set titles, librarians let each child choose any books that interested them and set a personal reading goal. Participation rose by 40 percent that summer, and librarians credited the freedom of choice for keeping children engaged week after week.","question":"What is the main idea of the passage?","choices":{"A":"Letting children choose their own books increased participation in the library's summer reading program.","B":"Public libraries should stop buying new books for children.","C":"Requiring set titles is the best way to keep children reading all summer.","D":"Librarians set personal reading goals for themselves in 2023."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The whole passage describes the change to free choice and the resulting 40 percent rise in participation, which librarians credited to that freedom."}},{"id":"sat_rw_09","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"Standard English Conventions","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"The committee, along with its outside advisors, ______ scheduled to present its final report to the city council on Tuesday.","question":"Which choice completes the sentence so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":{"A":"is","B":"are","C":"were","D":"have been"},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The subject is the singular 'committee'; the phrase 'along with its outside advisors' does not make the subject plural, so the singular verb 'is' is correct."}},{"id":"sat_rw_10","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"Standard English Conventions","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"After months of careful work, the museum's conservators were finally able to display the portrait again. The painting had regained ______ original colors, which had faded under decades of harsh light.","question":"Which choice completes the sentence so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":{"A":"it's","B":"their","C":"its","D":"its'"},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The possessive pronoun 'its' shows that the colors belong to the painting. 'It's' means 'it is,' and 'its'' is not a word."}},{"id":"sat_rw_11","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"Standard English Conventions","difficulty":"Hard","question":{"paragraph":"Walking through the crowded market, the smell of fresh bread and spices surrounded Elena and made her slow down to take it all in.","question":"Which choice revises the sentence so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":{"A":"Walking through the crowded market, the smell of fresh bread and spices surrounded Elena.","B":"Walking through the crowded market, Elena was surrounded by the smell of fresh bread and spices.","C":"Walking through the crowded market, the fresh bread and spices surrounded Elena with their smell.","D":"Walking through the crowded market, Elena's senses were surrounded by the smell of fresh bread and spices."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The opening phrase 'Walking through the crowded market' must describe the subject of the sentence. Only choice B makes Elena, the person walking, the subject."}},{"id":"sat_rw_12","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"Expression of Ideas","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"Renewable energy jobs have grown quickly in the region over the past decade. ______, some local schools still offer no training programs for the technical skills those jobs require.","question":"Which transition word or phrase best completes the sentence?","choices":{"A":"In addition","B":"For example","C":"Meanwhile","D":"However"},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"The second sentence contrasts with the first: jobs grew, yet training programs are lacking. 'However' signals that contrast."}},{"id":"sat_rw_13","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"Expression of Ideas","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"A paragraph in an essay argues that school gardens teach students responsibility. The writer wants to add a sentence that supports this claim with a concrete example.","question":"Which sentence best supports the claim that school gardens teach responsibility?","choices":{"A":"Students who sign up to water the garden each morning must arrive on time, even during exam week.","B":"School gardens are usually planted in the spring.","C":"Many students say vegetables taste better when they are fresh.","D":"Gardens have been part of schools for more than a century."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Only choice A gives a concrete example of responsibility: students must show up on time every morning to water the garden."}},{"id":"sat_rw_14","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"Expression of Ideas","difficulty":"Hard","question":{"paragraph":"A student is writing about urban beekeeping and takes the following notes: Rooftop hives are now kept on several city buildings; bees travel up to three miles from a hive to find flowers; a single rooftop hive can produce about 40 pounds of honey a year; city flowers bloom at different times across the season.","question":"The student wants to emphasize that rooftop hives can be surprisingly productive. Which choice best accomplishes that goal?","choices":{"A":"Bees from a rooftop hive may fly as far as three miles in search of flowers.","B":"City flowers bloom at different times throughout the season.","C":"One rooftop hive can produce about 40 pounds of honey in a single year.","D":"Rooftop hives are now kept on several buildings in the city."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The 40-pounds-of-honey detail directly shows productivity. The other choices concern travel distance, bloom timing, or how common the hives are."}},{"id":"sat_rw_15","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"Craft and Structure","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"The workshop introduced a novel approach to repairing phone screens: instead of replacing the whole display, technicians learned to mend the cracked layer alone, a method none of them had seen before.","question":"In the sentence, the word 'novel' most nearly means","choices":{"A":"a long work of fiction.","B":"new and unusual.","C":"famous and widely praised.","D":"difficult to understand."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The passage explains the approach is one 'none of them had seen before,' so 'novel' means new and unusual, not a book."}},{"id":"sat_rw_16","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"Craft and Structure","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"The author of a passage about deep-sea fish first describes how dark and cold the deep ocean is, then explains how one species, the anglerfish, solves the problem of finding food in that darkness with a glowing lure, and finally notes what scientists still hope to learn about the species.","question":"The main purpose of the passage's structure is to","choices":{"A":"argue that scientists should stop studying the deep ocean.","B":"compare the anglerfish with fish that live near the surface.","C":"list the names of every species that lives in the deep ocean.","D":"describe a challenging environment, explain how a species copes with it, and identify open questions."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"The passage moves from problem (a dark, cold habitat) to solution (the glowing lure) to remaining questions, which is exactly what choice D states."}},{"id":"sat_rw_17","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"Craft and Structure","difficulty":"Hard","question":{"paragraph":"In a passage about a drought-stricken farming town, the author writes: 'By July, the reservoir held less water than it had in any summer on record.' The next paragraph describes how families in the town began sharing well water and repairing leaks together.","question":"In context, the quoted sentence primarily serves to","choices":{"A":"establish how severe the drought was before showing how the town responded.","B":"suggest that keeping records caused the drought.","C":"argue that the town should build a larger reservoir.","D":"introduce a family that repairs leaks for a living."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The sentence gives a record-setting fact that establishes the drought's severity, setting up the following paragraph about the town's response."}},{"id":"sat_math_23","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Algebra","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"A gym charges a one-time joining fee plus a monthly fee. The total cost C, in dollars, for m months of membership is given by C = 15m + 40.","question":"In this equation, what does the number 40 represent?","choices":{"A":"The total cost of one year of membership","B":"The one-time joining fee","C":"The monthly fee","D":"The number of months in a year"},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The term 15m grows with the number of months (so 15 is the monthly fee), while 40 is the fixed amount paid once: the joining fee."}},{"id":"sat_math_24","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Algebra","difficulty":"Hard","question":{"paragraph":"If 2x + y = 11 and x - y = 4, where x and y are numbers that satisfy both equations.","question":"What is the value of x + y?","choices":{"A":"4","B":"5","C":"6","D":"7"},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Adding the equations gives 3x = 15, so x = 5. Then y = 11 - 2(5) = 1, so x + y = 5 + 1 = 6."}},{"id":"sat_math_25","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Advanced Math & Data","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"The graph of the quadratic function f(x) = x² - 8x + 3 is a parabola.","question":"What is the x-coordinate of the vertex of the parabola?","choices":{"A":"-4","B":"2","C":"8","D":"4"},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"For f(x) = x² - 8x + 3, the vertex has x = -b / (2a) = 8 / 2 = 4."}},{"id":"sat_math_26","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Advanced Math & Data","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"A data set consists of the five numbers 3, 7, 7, 10, and 13.","question":"What is the median of the data set?","choices":{"A":"7","B":"8","C":"7.5","D":"10"},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"In order, the numbers are 3, 7, 7, 10, 13. The middle (third) value is 7, so the median is 7."}},{"id":"sat_math_27","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Geometry & Trig","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"Two angles are supplementary, meaning their measures add up to 180 degrees. One of the angles measures 35 degrees.","question":"What is the measure, in degrees, of the other angle?","choices":{"A":"55","B":"135","C":"145","D":"155"},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Supplementary angles sum to 180 degrees: 180 - 35 = 145 degrees."}},{"id":"sat_math_28","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Geometry & Trig","difficulty":"Hard","question":{"paragraph":"A circle has a circumference of 20π units. The circumference of a circle is given by C = 2πr, where r is the radius.","question":"What is the radius, in units, of the circle?","choices":{"A":"5","B":"10","C":"20","D":"40"},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"20π = 2πr, so r = 20π / (2π) = 10 units."}},{"id":"sci_stem_02","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"Science & Biology","difficulty":"Easy","question":{"paragraph":"Photosynthesis is the process by which green plants use sunlight, water, and carbon dioxide to produce oxygen and glucose, the sugar the plant uses for energy and growth.","question":"What do plants produce during photosynthesis that they use for energy and growth?","choices":{"A":"Oxygen and glucose, and the glucose is what the plant uses for energy and growth","B":"Carbon dioxide and sunlight","C":"Only water, which the plant stores in its roots","D":"Pollen for other plants"},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The passage states that photosynthesis produces oxygen and glucose, and that the sugar (glucose) is what the plant uses for energy and growth."}},{"id":"sci_stem_03","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"Science & Biology","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"In a grassland food web, grasses make their own food from sunlight. Grasshoppers eat the grasses, frogs eat the grasshoppers, and herons eat the frogs.","question":"In this food web, which organism is a producer?","choices":{"A":"The grasshopper, because it produces energy for the frog","B":"The frog, because it produces food for the heron","C":"The heron, because it is at the top of the web","D":"The grasses, because they make their own food from sunlight"},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Producers make their own food. The passage says the grasses make their own food from sunlight, so the grasses are the producers."}},{"id":"sci_stem_04","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"Science & Biology","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"On a warm morning, puddles left by overnight rain gradually shrink and disappear, even though no one drains them. The liquid water is turning into water vapor and mixing into the air.","question":"The process by which liquid water turns into water vapor is called","choices":{"A":"condensation.","B":"evaporation.","C":"precipitation.","D":"freezing."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Evaporation is the change of liquid water into water vapor, which is why the puddles disappear into the air."}},{"id":"sci_stem_05","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"Science & Biology","difficulty":"Hard","question":{"paragraph":"In pea plants, the allele for tall stems (T) is dominant over the allele for short stems (t). Two parent plants that are each heterozygous (Tt) are crossed, so their offspring can be TT, Tt, or tt.","question":"Based on the cross, what percentage of the offspring are expected to be short?","choices":{"A":"0%","B":"50%","C":"25%","D":"75%"},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"A Tt × Tt cross gives roughly 1 TT : 2 Tt : 1 tt. Only the tt offspring (about 1 in 4, or 25%) show the recessive short trait."}},{"id":"kid_spell_02","targetProfile":"kid","domain":"6+ Spelling Bee","difficulty":"Easy","question":{"paragraph":"🏫 Phonics Clue: It starts with 'SCH' and it is the place where you learn with your teacher and friends!","question":"Which is the correct spelling for the word 'SCHOOL'?","choices":{"A":"SCOOL","B":"SCHOOL","C":"SCHOLL","D":"SKOOL"},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"S - C - H - O - O - L. Remember: SCHOOL has 'CH' after the S and two O's in the middle!"}},{"id":"kid_spell_03","targetProfile":"kid","domain":"6+ Spelling Bee","difficulty":"Easy","question":{"paragraph":"🐘 Memory Clue: Big Elephants Can Always Understand Small Elephants — the first letters spell this word!","question":"Which is the correct spelling for the word 'BECAUSE'?","choices":{"A":"BECUZ","B":"BECAUS","C":"BECASE","D":"BECAUSE"},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"B - E - C - A - U - S - E. The elephant sentence helps you remember every letter: Big Elephants Can Always Understand Small Elephants!"}},{"id":"kid_spell_04","targetProfile":"kid","domain":"6+ Spelling Bee","difficulty":"Easy","question":{"paragraph":"❤️ Word Clue: It starts with 'FAM' and means the people you love and live with!","question":"Which is the correct spelling for the word 'FAMILY'?","choices":{"A":"FAMILY","B":"FAMLY","C":"FAMILI","D":"FAMILEY"},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"F - A - M - I - L - Y. FAMILY ends with I-L-Y — say it slowly: FAM-i-ly!"}},{"id":"kid_spell_05","targetProfile":"kid","domain":"6+ Spelling Bee","difficulty":"Easy","question":{"paragraph":"💛 Color Clue: It starts with 'YELL' and it is the color of the sun and bananas!","question":"Which is the correct spelling for the word 'YELLOW'?","choices":{"A":"YELOW","B":"YELLOH","C":"YELLOW","D":"YELLLOW"},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Y - E - L - L - O - W. YELLOW has two L's and ends with W, like in 'the sun says hello, YELLOW!'"}},{"id":"kid_comp_02","targetProfile":"kid","domain":"6+ Story Comprehension","difficulty":"Easy","question":{"paragraph":"🌱 Mia planted a tiny seed in a pot. Every morning she gave it a little water and put it in the sunny window. After many days, a green sprout popped up, and Mia clapped her hands.","question":"Why did Mia's seed grow into a sprout?","choices":{"A":"Because she sang to it every night.","B":"Because she painted the pot green.","C":"Because she kept the pot in a dark closet.","D":"Because she gave it water and sunlight."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"The story says Mia gave the seed a little water every morning and put it in the sunny window — seeds need water and sunlight to grow!"}},{"id":"kid_comp_03","targetProfile":"kid","domain":"6+ Story Comprehension","difficulty":"Easy","question":{"paragraph":"🐾 Sam's puppy, Biscuit, ran out of the garden gate. Sam did not cry. He looked at the ground and saw little muddy paw prints on the path. He followed the prints all the way to the park, and there was Biscuit, waiting by the swings!","question":"How did Sam find Biscuit?","choices":{"A":"He followed the muddy paw prints on the path.","B":"He called Biscuit's name one time.","C":"He waited at home until Biscuit came back alone.","D":"He asked a bird where Biscuit went."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The story says Sam saw little muddy paw prints and followed them all the way to the park, where Biscuit was waiting!"}},{"id":"kid_comp_04","targetProfile":"kid","domain":"6+ Story Comprehension","difficulty":"Easy","question":{"paragraph":"🐢 Tim the Turtle was very slow, but he never stopped walking. Freddy the Frog hopped super fast, then sat down for a long nap because he thought he had lots of time. Tim kept going, step by step, and reached the finish line first!","question":"Why did Tim reach the finish line first?","choices":{"A":"Because frogs cannot hop to a finish line.","B":"Because he kept walking step by step and never stopped for a long nap.","C":"Because he rode on a bicycle.","D":"Because Freddy carried him there."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Freddy stopped for a long nap, but Tim kept going step by step without stopping — slow and steady won the race!"}},{"id":"kid_comp_05","targetProfile":"kid","domain":"6+ Story Comprehension","difficulty":"Easy","question":{"paragraph":"❄️ Ana woke up and looked out of her window. The whole garden was covered in white, fluffy snow! She jumped up and down, clapped her hands, and ran to find her warm red boots.","question":"How did Ana feel when she saw the snow?","choices":{"A":"Sleepy, because she wanted to stay in bed.","B":"Angry, because she could not find her boots.","C":"Excited and happy to play in the snow.","D":"Scared of the white garden."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Ana jumped up and down and clapped her hands — that shows she felt excited and happy about the snow!"}},{"id":"kid_gram_02","targetProfile":"kid","domain":"6+ Grammar & Sentences","difficulty":"Easy","question":{"paragraph":"🐾 Grammar Tip: A NOUN is a word that names a person, place, animal, or thing (like teacher, park, cat, or ball).","question":"Which word in this sentence is a NOUN?\n'The happy dog ran to the park.'","choices":{"A":"ran","B":"happy","C":"dog","D":"quickly"},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"'Dog' is the noun because it names an animal. 'Ran' is an action, and 'happy' describes the dog."}},{"id":"kid_gram_03","targetProfile":"kid","domain":"6+ Grammar & Sentences","difficulty":"Easy","question":{"paragraph":"🦊 Grammar Tip: When a word ends with X, we usually add -ES to talk about more than one (one box, two boxes).","question":"Which is the correct way to write more than one FOX?","choices":{"A":"foxes","B":"foxs","C":"foxies","D":"fox"},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"FOX ends with X, so we add -ES: one fox, two foxes! Just like one box, two boxes."}},{"id":"kid_gram_04","targetProfile":"kid","domain":"6+ Grammar & Sentences","difficulty":"Easy","question":{"paragraph":"🎨 Grammar Tip: A DESCRIBING WORD (adjective) tells us more about a noun, like its color, size, or how it feels.","question":"Which word in this sentence is the DESCRIBING WORD?\n'The soft cat sat on the mat.'","choices":{"A":"cat","B":"sat","C":"mat","D":"soft"},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"'Soft' is the describing word because it tells us how the cat feels. 'Cat' and 'mat' are nouns, and 'sat' is an action."}},{"id":"kid_gram_05","targetProfile":"kid","domain":"6+ Grammar & Sentences","difficulty":"Easy","question":{"paragraph":"❓ Grammar Tip: A sentence that asks something is a QUESTION, and a question ends with a question mark (?).","question":"Which sentence is written correctly?","choices":{"A":"where is my book.","B":"Where is my book?","C":"Where is my book","D":"where is my Book?"},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"'Where is my book?' starts with a capital letter and ends with a question mark because it asks a question!"}},{"id":"kid_ai_01","targetProfile":"kid","domain":"6+ AI Basics","difficulty":"Easy","question":{"paragraph":"Mia has a robot puppy named Bolt. Bolt can bark, wag his tail, and come when Mia calls his name. Bolt is a machine, but he almost seems alive!","question":"What is Bolt?","choices":{"A":"A robot — a machine built to sense, decide, and act","B":"A real puppy","C":"A stuffed toy with nothing inside","D":"A cartoon on a TV show"},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Robots are machines that can sense the world, make simple choices, and move or act — just like Bolt!"}},{"id":"kid_ai_02","targetProfile":"kid","domain":"6+ AI Basics","difficulty":"Easy","question":{"paragraph":"AI is short for Artificial Intelligence. You may have seen these two letters on tablets, speakers, and games.","question":"What does 'AI' stand for?","choices":{"A":"A type of animal","B":"Computer programs that can learn and make choices","C":"A board game","D":"A toy robot only"},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"AI means Artificial Intelligence: computer programs that can learn from examples and make choices."}},{"id":"kid_ai_03","targetProfile":"kid","domain":"6+ AI Basics","difficulty":"Easy","question":{"paragraph":"Before a spelling test, Ana practises her words many times so she can remember them. An AI learns in a similar way — people show it many, many examples so it can get better and better.","question":"How does an AI learn?","choices":{"A":"By taking a nap","B":"By guessing one time","C":"By looking at lots of examples, which is called training","D":"By reading a single page"},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"AI learns through training: studying lots of examples, the same way practising helps you learn!"}},{"id":"kid_ai_04","targetProfile":"kid","domain":"6+ AI Basics","difficulty":"Easy","question":{"paragraph":"When Dad says, 'Hey assistant, play my favourite song!', the smart speaker listens to his voice and finds the song right away.","question":"What kind of AI is Dad talking to?","choices":{"A":"A voice assistant — AI that listens to speech","B":"A magic mirror","C":"A regular radio","D":"A telephone book"},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"A voice assistant is AI that hears your words, understands them, and answers or helps."}},{"id":"kid_ai_05","targetProfile":"kid","domain":"6+ AI Basics","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"A photo app can find every picture of a cat in a family album of hundreds of photos in just one second. It knows what cats look like — pointy ears, whiskers, and furry faces.","question":"What is the AI in the photo app really good at finding?","choices":{"A":"Lost socks","B":"The weather tomorrow","C":"Hidden treasure","D":"Patterns — clues that repeat in pictures"},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"AI is super at spotting patterns: repeating clues like ears and whiskers tell it 'this is a cat!'"}},{"id":"kid_ai_06","targetProfile":"kid","domain":"6+ AI Basics","difficulty":"Easy","question":{"paragraph":"Tom asked a chatbot for help with his homework. The chatbot sounded very sure of itself, but one fact in its answer was wrong!","question":"What should Tom remember about AI answers?","choices":{"A":"AI is always right","B":"AI can make mistakes, so check important answers with a teacher or grown-up","C":"Chatbots know everything","D":"Wrong answers never happen"},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Even smart AI can make mistakes. For important things, always double-check with a trusted grown-up."}},{"id":"kid_ai_07","targetProfile":"kid","domain":"6+ AI Basics","difficulty":"Easy","question":{"paragraph":"Pictures, words, numbers, and sounds are all kinds of information. AI learns from information, just like you learn from books and teachers.","question":"What is 'data'?","choices":{"A":"A kind of dance","B":"A robot's battery","C":"Information, like pictures, words, and numbers","D":"The last level of a game"},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Data is information — pictures, words, numbers, and sounds that AI (and you!) can learn from."}},{"id":"kid_ai_08","targetProfile":"kid","domain":"6+ AI Basics","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"A self-driving car needs to 'see' the road to stay safe. It uses cameras and special sensors to spot people, other cars, and traffic lights all around it.","question":"What do the car's cameras and sensors do?","choices":{"A":"Help the car sense and understand the world around it","B":"Take holiday photos","C":"Play music for passengers","D":"Wash the windows"},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Sensors are like eyes and ears for machines — they help the car notice everything around it."}},{"id":"kid_ai_09","targetProfile":"kid","domain":"6+ AI Basics","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"Sara loves videos about volcanoes. Lately, her video app keeps putting more and more volcano videos on her home screen, before she even searches for them.","question":"Why does the app show Sara so many volcano videos?","choices":{"A":"The app is broken","B":"Volcanoes are the only videos in the world","C":"AI noticed what Sara likes and recommends more of it","D":"A button got stuck"},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Recommendation AI learns what you enjoy watching and suggests more of it. That's helpful — but remember to explore new things too!"}},{"id":"kid_ai_10","targetProfile":"kid","domain":"6+ AI Basics","difficulty":"Easy","question":{"paragraph":"Leo is chatting with a friendly chatbot. Suddenly it asks, 'Where do you live? Tell me your home address!' Leo remembers what his teacher taught him about staying safe online.","question":"What should Leo do?","choices":{"A":"Type his home address","B":"Keep personal information private and tell a grown-up","C":"Share his password too","D":"Tell the chatbot his school's name"},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Never share private information like your address or password with a chatbot. Tell a grown-up when something feels odd."}},{"id":"teen_ai_01","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"AI & Technology","difficulty":"Easy","question":{"paragraph":"Machine learning is a branch of artificial intelligence. Instead of following only fixed rules written by programmers, a machine learning system improves its performance on a task by analysing data.","question":"What is the main idea of the passage?","choices":{"A":"Programmers write every rule an AI system follows","B":"Machine learning systems improve by learning from data","C":"Artificial intelligence and machine learning are unrelated fields","D":"Data analysis is only useful for statistics classes"},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The passage contrasts fixed rules with learning from data — machine learning systems improve their performance by analysing data."}},{"id":"teen_ai_02","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"AI & Technology","difficulty":"Easy","question":{"paragraph":"A neural network is a computing model inspired by the human brain. It consists of layers of connected units, sometimes called artificial neurons, which pass numbers to one another. During training, the system adjusts the strength of these connections so that its outputs become more accurate.","question":"According to the passage, a neural network becomes more accurate by","choices":{"A":"adding more layers every time it makes an error","B":"copying the structure of the brain exactly","C":"adjusting the strength of connections between its units during training","D":"storing every example it has ever seen"},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The passage says training adjusts the strength of the connections between units, which improves accuracy."}},{"id":"teen_ai_03","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"AI & Technology","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"Large language models, the technology behind modern chatbots, are trained on enormous collections of text. When generating a reply, the model does not retrieve a stored answer; instead, it calculates which word (or word fragment) is most likely to come next, adds it, and repeats the process until the response is complete.","question":"How does a large language model produce a response?","choices":{"A":"It searches a database for a pre-written answer","B":"It repeatedly predicts the most likely next word or fragment","C":"It copies sentences from its training texts in order","D":"It asks a human operator for each sentence"},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"An LLM generates text step by step, each time predicting the most probable next token given everything so far."}},{"id":"teen_ai_04","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"AI & Technology","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"Training is only the first stage in an AI system's life. Engineers first adjust the model using historical data; afterwards, the finished model is deployed to make predictions about brand-new cases it has never seen. This second stage is known as inference.","question":"In the passage, 'inference' refers to","choices":{"A":"the process of collecting training data","B":"a model's first round of training","C":"engineers discussing a model's design","D":"a trained model making predictions on new cases"},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Inference is the deployment stage: the already-trained model predicts outcomes for new, unseen inputs."}},{"id":"teen_ai_05","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"AI & Technology","difficulty":"Hard","question":{"paragraph":"A student trains a model on a set of past exam questions until it answers those exact questions perfectly. When the same model is given a brand-new exam covering the same topics, however, its score falls dramatically.","question":"The model's weakness is best described as","choices":{"A":"overfitting — it memorised the training questions instead of learning general patterns","B":"underfitting — it was not trained long enough","C":"hallucination — it invented questions that do not exist","D":"bias — the exam topics were unfair"},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Scoring perfectly on training data but failing on new data is the classic signature of overfitting: memorisation without generalisation."}},{"id":"teen_ai_06","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"AI & Technology","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"A large company built a hiring tool trained on ten years of its recruitment records. Because the company had historically hired mostly men for engineering roles, the tool learned from those records to rank male applicants more favourably — even though gender was never given to it directly.","question":"The hiring tool's behaviour is primarily an example of","choices":{"A":"a programming syntax error","B":"bias inherited from the data the model was trained on","C":"overfitting to a single applicant","D":"a model refusing to make predictions"},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Models learn the patterns in their training data — including historical biases, which they can then reproduce at scale."}},{"id":"teen_ai_07","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"AI & Technology","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"Researchers use a specific term when a chatbot states something false with complete confidence — for instance, summarising in detail a scientific paper that was never written, complete with invented authors and results.","question":"The phenomenon described is known as","choices":{"A":"tokenisation","B":"inference","C":"hallucination","D":"prompt engineering"},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Confident but fabricated output from a language model is called a hallucination — which is why AI answers should be verified."}},{"id":"teen_ai_08","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"AI & Technology","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"In supervised learning, every training example comes with the correct answer attached: photographs labelled 'cat' or 'dog', essays marked with grades. In unsupervised learning, the system receives no labels at all and must discover groups or patterns in the data by itself.","question":"What distinguishes supervised from unsupervised learning?","choices":{"A":"Supervised learning uses neural networks; unsupervised learning does not","B":"Supervised learning is always more accurate","C":"Unsupervised learning requires more data","D":"Supervised learning trains on examples that include the correct answers"},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"The defining feature of supervised learning is labelled data — examples paired with their correct answers."}},{"id":"teen_ai_09","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"AI & Technology","difficulty":"Easy","question":{"paragraph":"Computer vision systems can scan thousands of chest X-rays and flag the scans most likely to show early signs of disease. Radiologists then review the flagged cases first, reaching the patients who need help soonest.","question":"The passage describes computer vision being used to","choices":{"A":"analyse medical images and prioritise cases for doctors","B":"replace doctors entirely","C":"design new hospital buildings","D":"translate medical records into other languages"},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The system analyses X-ray images and flags likely cases so doctors can review them first — an assistive, not replacement, role."}},{"id":"teen_ai_10","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"AI & Technology","difficulty":"Easy","question":{"paragraph":"In 1950, computer pioneer Alan Turing proposed a practical test of machine intelligence. A human judge exchanges written messages with both a person and a machine, without knowing which is which. If the judge cannot reliably tell the machine from the person, the machine is said to pass the test.","question":"According to the passage, a machine passes the Turing test when","choices":{"A":"it can defeat a chess champion","B":"a judge cannot reliably distinguish it from a human in conversation","C":"it answers every question correctly","D":"it builds another machine"},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Turing's criterion is conversational indistinguishability: the judge cannot reliably tell machine from human."}},{"id":"teen_ai_11","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"AI & Technology","difficulty":"Easy","question":{"paragraph":"The instructions given to a chatbot strongly shape its answers. 'Explain photosynthesis' produces a generic paragraph, while 'Explain photosynthesis to a ten-year-old using a kitchen analogy' produces something quite different. The skill of crafting such effective instructions is known as prompt engineering.","question":"Prompt engineering is best defined as","choices":{"A":"programming a chatbot's source code","B":"repairing computer hardware","C":"writing clear, effective instructions for an AI system","D":"training a model on new data"},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Prompt engineering is about the instructions you give: well-crafted prompts steer the AI toward the answer you actually need."}},{"id":"teen_ai_12","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"AI & Technology","difficulty":"Hard","question":{"paragraph":"Deepfake technology can now generate highly realistic fake videos of real people saying things they never said. Experts argue that no single defence is sufficient: detection software, platform policies, laws, and a public habit of verifying surprising videos before sharing them must all work together.","question":"Which conclusion is best supported by the passage?","choices":{"A":"Detection software alone will solve the deepfake problem","B":"Fake videos are impossible to identify","C":"Laws have no role in addressing deepfakes","D":"Defending against deepfakes requires a combination of technology, policy, and public scepticism"},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"The passage explicitly rejects any single defence and calls for technology, policy, and verification habits together."}},{"id":"sat_rw_18","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"Information and Ideas","difficulty":"Easy","question":{"paragraph":"Marine biologists working off the coast of Florida have begun \"planting\" coral the way gardeners plant seedlings. Divers attach small fragments of healthy coral to underwater frames, where the fragments grow into new colonies. In areas where the method has been used for several years, fish populations have rebounded, and the reefs have become more resistant to storm damage.","question":"What is the main idea of the passage?","choices":{"A":"A technique for growing new coral from fragments is helping damaged reefs recover.","B":"Fish populations off the coast of Florida have declined in recent years.","C":"Storms are the greatest threat facing coral reefs today.","D":"Divers in Florida prefer working underwater to working in gardens."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The passage describes the fragment-planting method and its results (rebounding fish and hardier reefs), so choice A captures the main idea. The other choices are contradicted by or absent from the passage."}},{"id":"sat_rw_19","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"Information and Ideas","difficulty":"Easy","question":{"paragraph":"Peregrine falcons, once endangered, have found an unlikely home in large cities. The tall buildings provide ledges that resemble the cliff faces where the birds naturally nest, and the city streets below teem with pigeons, a favorite prey. Several cities now host nesting pairs on skyscrapers, and some buildings even maintain webcams so the public can watch the chicks hatch.","question":"According to the passage, peregrine falcons thrive in cities partly because","choices":{"A":"webcams allow the public to watch their chicks hatch.","B":"tall buildings offer nesting ledges similar to natural cliffs.","C":"the birds have no natural predators in urban areas.","D":"pigeons nest on the same ledges as the falcons."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The passage states that tall buildings provide ledges resembling the cliff faces where falcons naturally nest. The webcams are mentioned only as a public feature, and the other choices are not stated."}},{"id":"sat_rw_20","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"Information and Ideas","difficulty":"Easy","question":{"paragraph":"At the regional robotics competition, the team from Westfield High watched its robot fail its first match when the gripper dropped a game piece. That evening, the students stayed late to rebuild the gripper with wider, rubber-coated fingers. The next day, the redesigned robot completed every task, and Westfield finished in second place.","question":"According to the passage, what did the Westfield team do after its robot failed the first match?","choices":{"A":"It withdrew from the competition.","B":"It borrowed a gripper from another team.","C":"It rebuilt the gripper with wider, rubber-coated fingers.","D":"It asked the judges to replay the match."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The passage says the students stayed late to rebuild the gripper with wider, rubber-coated fingers, which is exactly choice C. None of the other actions are mentioned."}},{"id":"sat_rw_21","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"Information and Ideas","difficulty":"Easy","question":{"paragraph":"At a research station in Antarctica, winter brings four months of darkness and storms that make travel impossible. Because no planes can reach the station during that season, the crew stores a full winter's worth of food, fuel, and medicine in advance. Whatever the researchers forget to stock, they must simply do without until spring.","question":"According to the passage, why do researchers at the station store supplies in advance?","choices":{"A":"The crew prefers to avoid flying during the winter.","B":"Food and fuel are cheaper when bought in large amounts.","C":"The station's storage buildings are only open before winter.","D":"Storms and darkness make it impossible for planes to reach the station in winter."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"The passage explains that winter storms and darkness make travel impossible, so no planes can deliver supplies. Choice D restates that reason; the other choices are never mentioned."}},{"id":"sat_rw_22","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"Information and Ideas","difficulty":"Easy","question":{"paragraph":"When the freight railroad through Millbrook was abandoned, the town converted the old rail bed into a paved walking and cycling trail. Within a year, residents were using the trail daily, two new cafés opened near its entrance, and the town reported a noticeable rise in visitors on weekends.","question":"What is the main idea of the passage?","choices":{"A":"Turning an abandoned railroad into a trail benefited the town of Millbrook in several ways.","B":"Freight railroads have become less important than they once were.","C":"Cycling is the most popular form of exercise in Millbrook.","D":"Towns should pave all of their walking paths."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Every detail in the passage (daily use, new cafés, more visitors) shows a benefit of converting the rail bed into a trail, which is choice A. The other choices are unsupported or go beyond the passage."}},{"id":"sat_rw_23","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"Information and Ideas","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"Coastal towns in Bangladesh have been planting mangrove forests along their shorelines. A mangrove's dense tangle of roots slows down waves driven by storms, reducing the flooding that reaches homes behind the forest. The roots also trap sediment, gradually building up the shoreline, and they shelter young fish and crabs.","question":"Based on the passage, the towns plant mangroves primarily in order to","choices":{"A":"create habitats where young fish can be harvested for food.","B":"lessen the flooding that storm waves cause in the towns.","C":"stop storm waves from reaching the shoreline at all.","D":"replace farmland that has been lost to the sea."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The passage emphasizes that mangrove roots slow storm waves, 'reducing the flooding that reaches homes,' which matches choice B. Choice C overstates the effect, and the shelter provided to young fish is a side benefit, not a harvest program."}},{"id":"sat_rw_24","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"Information and Ideas","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"Historian Elena Vasquez argues that the printing press changed Europe less by creating new ideas than by changing who could afford to encounter them. Before the press, a single handwritten book could cost as much as a house; within decades of the press's invention, printed texts cost a fraction of that. Vasquez notes that literacy rates climbed fastest in the regions where cheap books arrived earliest.","question":"What is the main point of Vasquez's argument as presented in the passage?","choices":{"A":"The printing press was invented for the purpose of making books cheaper.","B":"Europeans had few new ideas before the printing press was invented.","C":"The press's greatest effect was to widen access to books by sharply lowering their cost.","D":"Literacy rates rose mainly because new schools were built across Europe."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Vasquez's claim is that the press mattered most by changing 'who could afford' books, and the cost and literacy details support that point, as choice C states. Schools and the press's intended purpose are never mentioned."}},{"id":"sat_rw_25","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"Information and Ideas","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"In an experiment, octopuses were given clear jars with screw-top lids and a crab inside. On the first attempt, most octopuses needed more than ten minutes to open a jar. By the fifth trial, however, the same animals routinely opened the jars in under a minute, and they used the same twisting motion each time.","question":"The experiment described in the passage most strongly suggests that octopuses","choices":{"A":"prefer crabs to any other kind of food.","B":"can learn tasks only by watching other octopuses.","C":"are stronger than most other sea animals.","D":"can improve at a task through repeated experience."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"The octopuses went from needing ten minutes to under a minute across five trials, showing improvement with practice, as choice D says. Food preference, watching others, and strength are not addressed."}},{"id":"sat_rw_26","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"Information and Ideas","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"After beavers were reintroduced to a valley in Oregon, their dams slowed the stream and created a series of ponds. Within three years, surveys found that the number of juvenile trout in the valley had tripled; the still pond water gave young fish shelter from fast currents and predators. Some ranchers initially worried the dams would flood their pastures, but the water table rose instead, keeping wells fuller during dry months.","question":"According to the passage, one result of the beavers' return was that","choices":{"A":"the number of young trout in the valley tripled.","B":"ranchers' pastures were flooded by the new ponds.","C":"the stream flowed faster through the valley.","D":"wells in the valley began to run dry in summer."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The passage directly states that the number of juvenile trout tripled. The other choices contradict the text: the stream slowed, pastures were not flooded, and wells stayed fuller."}},{"id":"sat_rw_27","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"Information and Ideas","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"Children who grow up speaking two languages constantly decide which language a situation calls for and suppress the other. Psychologist Mara Lindqvist argues that this daily practice strengthens the brain's general system for controlling attention. In her studies, bilingual children outperformed monolingual children on puzzles that required ignoring misleading clues, even when the puzzles had nothing to do with language.","question":"Which choice best states the main claim Lindqvist makes in the passage?","choices":{"A":"Bilingual children learn to read earlier than monolingual children.","B":"Managing two languages strengthens children's general ability to control attention.","C":"Bilingual children perform better on puzzles than on language tasks.","D":"Children should be taught a second language before the age of five."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Lindqvist's claim is that juggling two languages strengthens the general attention-control system, and the puzzle study supports it. Choice B states that claim; reading age, puzzle-vs-language comparisons, and teaching recommendations are not in the passage."}},{"id":"sat_rw_28","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"Information and Ideas","difficulty":"Hard","question":{"paragraph":"Of the roughly 7,000 languages spoken today, linguists estimate that one falls silent about every two weeks, when its last speaker dies. Many communities are recording dictionaries and stories in endangered languages, and these archives are invaluable to researchers. Yet a language survives in the fullest sense only when children learn it at home and use it with one another; a language preserved only in recordings becomes an object of study rather than a living medium.","question":"The author of the passage most likely believes that recording an endangered language","choices":{"A":"is a waste of resources that could be spent on teaching.","B":"guarantees that the language will one day be spoken again.","C":"is valuable but cannot by itself keep the language alive.","D":"should be carried out only by trained linguists."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The author calls the archives 'invaluable' but insists a language fully survives only when children learn and use it, so recording alone is valuable yet insufficient, as choice C says. The passage neither dismisses recording nor guarantees revival."}},{"id":"sat_rw_29","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"Information and Ideas","difficulty":"Hard","question":{"paragraph":"Several desert towns have adopted \"dark sky\" ordinances that require outdoor lights to be shielded and pointed downward. Astronomers praised the rules, which keep artificial glare from washing out faint stars. Some residents at first objected that dimmer streets would be less safe, but the towns found that shielded lights actually improved visibility by reducing glare, and reported crime did not increase. Within a few years, the ordinances had won support from both observatories and neighborhood associations.","question":"Based on the passage, the ordinances succeeded in part because they","choices":{"A":"required residents to turn off all outdoor lights at night.","B":"made the town streets brighter than they had been before.","C":"were imposed on the towns by observatories over residents' objections.","D":"addressed residents' safety concerns while also reducing glare for astronomers."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"The ordinances won broad support because shielded lights both reduced glare for astronomers and improved visibility without increasing crime, answering the safety objection. Choice D captures both sides; the others contradict or distort the passage."}},{"id":"sat_rw_30","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"Information and Ideas","difficulty":"Hard","question":{"paragraph":"Economists describe a puzzle known as the paradox of thrift. When a downturn frightens households, each family reasonably decides to spend less and save more. But one family's spending is another's income; when millions act on the same sensible impulse at once, shops earn less, employers cut hours, and total savings across the economy can actually fall. What is prudent for one household, multiplied across all of them, can deepen the very slump everyone feared.","question":"Which choice best states the main argument of the passage?","choices":{"A":"Saving more is wise for an individual household but can harm the whole economy when everyone does it simultaneously.","B":"Households should never save money during an economic downturn.","C":"Economists are deeply divided over whether saving is good for the economy.","D":"Employers, not households, bear the main responsibility for economic slumps."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The passage's paradox is exactly choice A: individual prudence becomes collective harm when everyone saves at once. The passage does not forbid saving, report a deep division among economists, or blame employers."}},{"id":"sat_rw_31","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"Craft and Structure","difficulty":"Easy","question":{"paragraph":"For her geography project, Ana printed a special map of her state. On most maps, the land appears flat, but on Ana's map the relief was shaded in greens and browns, so viewers could see how the plains in the east give way to mountains in the west.","question":"In the passage, the word \"relief\" most nearly means","choices":{"A":"the feeling of being free from worry.","B":"the variations in height of the land.","C":"help given to people in need.","D":"a break from work or duty."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"On a map, 'relief' refers to the shape and height differences of the land, shown here by shading that reveals plains and mountains. The other meanings of relief do not fit a geography map."}},{"id":"sat_rw_32","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"Craft and Structure","difficulty":"Easy","question":{"paragraph":"Lighthouses warn ships away from dangerous rocks and guide them toward harbors. A lighthouse's lamp sits atop a tall tower so that its beam can be seen from far at sea. In the nineteenth century, French physicist Augustin Fresnel designed a lens of concentric rings that could focus a modest flame into a beam visible for miles.","question":"Why does the author mention Augustin Fresnel?","choices":{"A":"To explain why lighthouses were first built in France.","B":"To argue that lighthouses are no longer necessary.","C":"To give an example of an improvement in lighthouse technology.","D":"To describe how lighthouse keepers lived in the nineteenth century."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Fresnel's lens made a small flame visible for miles, so he is mentioned as an example of a technological improvement to lighthouses. The passage says nothing about keepers' lives or lighthouses becoming unnecessary."}},{"id":"sat_rw_33","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"Craft and Structure","difficulty":"Easy","question":{"paragraph":"Before carving the final sculpture, Maya made a draft out of clay. The small model let her test the pose and proportions; once she was satisfied with the draft, she began work on the full-size marble version.","question":"In the passage, the word \"draft\" most nearly means","choices":{"A":"a current of moving air.","B":"a written order for payment.","C":"the selection of players for a sports team.","D":"a preliminary version of a work."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"The clay model is a first, test version of the sculpture made before the final marble one, so 'draft' means a preliminary version. The other definitions belong to different contexts."}},{"id":"sat_rw_34","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"Craft and Structure","difficulty":"Easy","question":{"paragraph":"The author of a civics text explains how a bill becomes a law. First, a member of Congress introduces the bill. Next, a committee studies it and may revise it. Then both chambers vote on the final text, and if they approve it, the bill goes to the president for a signature.","question":"The structure of this text is best described as","choices":{"A":"a sequence of steps in a process.","B":"a comparison of two opposing arguments.","C":"a problem followed by several possible solutions.","D":"a detailed description of a single historical event."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The text proceeds with 'First... Next... Then...', laying out the stages of a process in order, which is a sequence of steps. No opposing arguments, problem-solution set, or single event is presented."}},{"id":"sat_rw_35","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"Craft and Structure","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"A sudden roll of thunder arrested the campers' conversation. Around the fire, everyone fell silent and listened as the sound rumbled away across the lake.","question":"In the passage, the word \"arrested\" most nearly means","choices":{"A":"seized by the police.","B":"captured and held.","C":"formally accused of a crime.","D":"delayed until a later time."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The thunder caught and held the campers' attention, stopping their conversation; 'arrested' here means captured and held. The law-enforcement meanings do not fit a sound holding people's attention."}},{"id":"sat_rw_36","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"Craft and Structure","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"When botanist June Park described the night-blooming desert plant her team had found, she needed a name for its unusual trait of opening only under a full moon. She coined the word \"lunabloom\" for the trait, and the term soon appeared in other researchers' papers.","question":"In the passage, the word \"coined\" most nearly means","choices":{"A":"stamped onto metal disks.","B":"earned a large amount of money by.","C":"invented or created.","D":"examined very carefully."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Park created a brand-new word, 'lunabloom,' so 'coined' means invented. The passage is about naming a trait, not about minting money or inspecting something."}},{"id":"sat_rw_37","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"Craft and Structure","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"In her workshop in Cremona, luthier Sofia Bernasconi keeps pigments ground by hand and spruce logs aged for ten years. She taps each piece of wood and listens to its ring before she carves it, rejecting any piece whose tone displeases her. The violins that emerge from this process are prized by musicians for their warm, carrying sound.","question":"The details about Bernasconi's workshop and methods primarily serve to","choices":{"A":"prove that Cremona produces more violins than any other city.","B":"explain why Bernasconi's violins are so expensive.","C":"compare Bernasconi's methods with those of other violin makers.","D":"suggest that the violins' celebrated sound results from painstaking craftsmanship."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"The hand-ground pigments, decade-aged wood, and careful listening all emphasize meticulous craft, and the passage links that care to the violins' prized sound. No price, production ranking, or comparison with other makers appears."}},{"id":"sat_rw_38","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"Craft and Structure","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"Many people assume that bats are blind, relying entirely on sound to find their way. In fact, all bat species can see, and some fruit bats have excellent eyesight. Echolocation is an additional tool, not a replacement for vision: most bats use both senses together, much as a person might use both a map and a flashlight.","question":"Which choice best describes the overall structure of the passage?","choices":{"A":"It presents a common assumption and then corrects it with evidence.","B":"It lists the hunting techniques of bats in order of importance.","C":"It argues that echolocation is more useful to bats than vision.","D":"It compares the eyesight of fruit bats with that of other mammals."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The passage opens with the popular assumption that bats are blind and then corrects it with facts about bat vision, matching choice A. It does not rank hunting techniques or argue that one sense is superior."}},{"id":"sat_rw_39","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"Craft and Structure","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"The novel alternates between two narrators: a lighthouse keeper's daughter writing in 1911 and a marine biologist writing a century later about the same island. Each describes the winter storm that damaged the lighthouse, but they disagree about a crucial point: whether the keeper left his post that night.","question":"The alternating narration primarily serves to","choices":{"A":"provide a complete history of the island's lighthouse.","B":"show that the same event can be understood in conflicting ways.","C":"explain how storms damage lighthouses.","D":"contrast everyday life in 1911 with everyday life today."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The two narrators describe the same storm but disagree about a key point, so the structure highlights how one event can be interpreted in conflicting ways. The passage focuses on their disagreement, not on a full history or daily-life contrast."}},{"id":"sat_rw_40","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"Craft and Structure","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"In a passage about the rocky shore, the author writes: \"When the tide withdraws, a barnacle snaps its shell plates shut, trapping a film of seawater inside until the ocean returns.\" The surrounding paragraphs discuss how shore creatures survive the dry hours between high tides.","question":"In context, the quoted sentence primarily serves to","choices":{"A":"argue that barnacles are the hardiest animals on the shore.","B":"introduce the topic of ocean tides.","C":"give a specific example of how a shore creature survives low tide.","D":"explain why the tide withdraws at certain times of day."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Within paragraphs about how shore creatures endure low tide, the sentence offers the barnacle as one concrete example of a survival strategy. It makes no ranking argument and does not explain tidal causes."}},{"id":"sat_rw_41","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"Craft and Structure","difficulty":"Hard","question":{"paragraph":"The review praised the young pianist's daring interpretation but tempered its praise by noting that her left hand sometimes overwhelmed the melody. Readers were left with a balanced, if not wholly enthusiastic, verdict.","question":"In the passage, the word \"tempered\" most nearly means","choices":{"A":"heated and hardened.","B":"expressed more strongly.","C":"repeated several times.","D":"moderated or softened."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"The review softened its praise with a criticism, producing a 'balanced' verdict, so 'tempered' means moderated. The metalworking sense ('heated and hardened') is a trap that does not fit criticism."}},{"id":"sat_rw_42","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"Craft and Structure","difficulty":"Hard","question":{"paragraph":"Examining the vase under magnification, the conservator found minute cracks in the glaze: fractures so fine that the naked eye had missed them for decades, though water had been slowly seeping through them all along.","question":"In the passage, the word \"minute\" most nearly means","choices":{"A":"extremely small.","B":"brief in duration.","C":"precisely recorded.","D":"of little importance."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The cracks are described as 'so fine that the naked eye had missed them,' so 'minute' (pronounced my-NOOT) means extremely small. Duration and record-keeping senses do not describe physical fractures."}},{"id":"sat_rw_43","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"Craft and Structure","difficulty":"Hard","question":{"paragraph":"Why do zebras have stripes? One hypothesis holds that the pattern confuses predators lunging into a herd; another proposes that stripes deter biting flies, which land less often on striped surfaces. Experiments support the fly idea consistently, while evidence for predator confusion remains mixed. Stripes may yet serve more than one purpose, but at present the fly hypothesis rests on the firmer ground.","question":"Which choice best describes the main purpose of the passage?","choices":{"A":"To prove that zebra stripes evolved for a single reason.","B":"To present two explanations for a natural pattern and evaluate the evidence for each.","C":"To argue that predators pose little threat to zebras.","D":"To describe a single experiment that settled a scientific debate."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The passage lays out two hypotheses about stripes and then weighs the evidence for each, concluding one is better supported. It explicitly leaves open multiple purposes and describes no single decisive experiment."}},{"id":"sat_rw_44","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"Standard English Conventions","difficulty":"Easy","question":{"paragraph":"The puppies in the wicker basket ______ fast asleep after their morning of play.","question":"Which choice completes the sentence so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":{"A":"is","B":"be","C":"are","D":"was"},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The subject 'puppies' is plural, so the sentence needs the plural present-tense verb 'are.' 'Is' and 'was' are singular, and 'be' is not a correct finite verb form here."}},{"id":"sat_rw_45","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"Standard English Conventions","difficulty":"Easy","question":{"paragraph":"Last weekend, Marcus ______ his first marathon, finishing in just under four hours.","question":"Which choice completes the sentence so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":{"A":"run","B":"runs","C":"running","D":"ran"},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"The phrase 'Last weekend' places the action in the past, so the past-tense verb 'ran' is required. The other forms are present tense or incomplete without an auxiliary verb."}},{"id":"sat_rw_46","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"Standard English Conventions","difficulty":"Easy","question":{"paragraph":"Before the championship game, the ______ new uniforms were laid out in a neat row in the locker room.","question":"Which choice completes the sentence so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":{"A":"players'","B":"players","C":"player's","D":"players's"},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The uniforms belong to all the players (plural), and a plural noun ending in -s forms its possessive with an apostrophe alone: 'players'.' 'Player's' would mean one player, and 'players's' is never correct."}},{"id":"sat_rw_47","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"Standard English Conventions","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"Neither the coach nor the players ______ aware that the game had been moved to Saturday.","question":"Which choice completes the sentence so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":{"A":"was","B":"were","C":"is","D":"has been"},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"With 'neither... nor,' the verb agrees with the nearer subject, 'players,' which is plural, so 'were' is correct. The past-tense context ('had been moved') also rules out the present-tense options."}},{"id":"sat_rw_48","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"Standard English Conventions","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"Every musician in the youth orchestra is responsible for tuning ______ own instrument before rehearsal begins.","question":"Which choice completes the sentence so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":{"A":"its","B":"they're","C":"their","D":"them"},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"A possessive determiner is needed before 'own instrument,' and 'their' is the possessive form that refers back to 'every musician.' 'Its' refers to things, 'they're' means 'they are,' and 'them' is an object pronoun."}},{"id":"sat_rw_49","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"Standard English Conventions","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"The recipe calls for washing the rice, soaking the beans, and ______ the vegetables before anything goes into the pot.","question":"Which choice completes the sentence so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":{"A":"chop","B":"to chop","C":"you chop","D":"chopping"},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Items in a series must be parallel in form. 'Washing' and 'soaking' are gerunds, so the third item must also be a gerund: 'chopping.' The other options break the parallel structure."}},{"id":"sat_rw_50","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"Standard English Conventions","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"Of the three routes up the mountain, the northern trail is ______ and also the most scenic.","question":"Which choice completes the sentence so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":{"A":"the steepest","B":"steeper","C":"more steepest","D":"most steep"},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Because three routes are being compared, the superlative form is required, matching 'the most scenic' later in the sentence. 'The steepest' is the correct superlative; 'more steepest' double-marks the comparison."}},{"id":"sat_rw_51","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"Standard English Conventions","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"The storm knocked out power across the entire county ______ thousands of residents spent the night without electricity.","question":"Which choice completes the sentence so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":{"A":"and","B":", and","C":", however","D":"because"},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The blank joins two independent clauses, which Standard English connects with a comma plus a coordinating conjunction: ', and.' A comma with 'however' creates a comma splice, and 'because' would reverse the cause-and-effect logic."}},{"id":"sat_rw_52","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"Standard English Conventions","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"The ______ wing was injured, so the wildlife center kept the hawk for a week before releasing it.","question":"Which choice completes the sentence so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":{"A":"birds","B":"birds'","C":"bird's","D":"birds's"},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The wing belongs to one bird (the hawk), so the singular possessive 'bird's' is correct. 'Birds'' would show possession by several birds, and 'birds's' is not a valid form."}},{"id":"sat_rw_53","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"Standard English Conventions","difficulty":"Hard","question":{"paragraph":"The city council recommended that the collected funds ______ used to repair the library roof.","question":"Which choice completes the sentence so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":{"A":"are","B":"were","C":"is","D":"be"},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"After verbs of recommendation or demand followed by 'that,' Standard English uses the subjunctive, which is the base form of the verb: 'be.' The other options are ordinary indicative forms and do not fit this construction."}},{"id":"sat_rw_54","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"Standard English Conventions","difficulty":"Hard","question":{"paragraph":"Between you and ______, this timetable leaves almost no room for delays.","question":"Which choice completes the sentence so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":{"A":"me","B":"I","C":"myself","D":"mine"},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"'Between' is a preposition, so the pronoun that follows it must be in the objective case: 'me.' The common phrase 'between you and I' is a well-known error in Standard English."}},{"id":"sat_rw_55","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"Standard English Conventions","difficulty":"Hard","question":{"paragraph":"By the time the firefighters arrived at the warehouse, the night crew ______ most of the inventory to safety.","question":"Which choice completes the sentence so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":{"A":"moves","B":"had moved","C":"will move","D":"is moving"},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The crew's action was completed before another past event (the firefighters' arrival), so the past perfect 'had moved' is required. The present and future forms clash with the past-tense frame set by 'arrived.'"}},{"id":"sat_rw_56","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"Expression of Ideas","difficulty":"Easy","question":{"paragraph":"Overnight, temperatures dropped well below freezing, and the water in the school's exposed pipes froze solid. ______, classes had to be canceled the next morning while plumbers made repairs.","question":"Which transition word or phrase best completes the sentence?","choices":{"A":"In contrast","B":"For example","C":"In addition","D":"As a result"},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"The canceled classes were a consequence of the frozen pipes, so the cause-and-effect transition 'As a result' fits. The other transitions signal contrast, example, or addition, none of which match the relationship."}},{"id":"sat_rw_57","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"Expression of Ideas","difficulty":"Easy","question":{"paragraph":"The community center offers free tutoring for students after school. ______, it runs a weekend art program that any resident can join.","question":"Which transition word or phrase best completes the sentence?","choices":{"A":"In addition","B":"However","C":"Otherwise","D":"For instance"},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The second sentence adds another service the center provides, so the additive transition 'In addition' is correct. The art program is not an example of tutoring, nor does it contrast with it."}},{"id":"sat_rw_58","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"Expression of Ideas","difficulty":"Easy","question":{"paragraph":"A paragraph in an essay argues that regular exercise can improve a person's mood. The writer wants to add a sentence that supports this claim with a concrete example.","question":"Which sentence best supports the claim that regular exercise improves mood?","choices":{"A":"Exercise equipment can be expensive if it is bought new.","B":"After joining a lunchtime walking group, Mr. Alvarez found that he felt calmer and more cheerful on workday afternoons.","C":"Many people find it difficult to exercise during the winter.","D":"The community gym is open from six in the morning until ten at night."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Choice B gives a concrete case of exercise (a walking group) leading to a better mood (calmer and more cheerful). The other sentences concern cost, difficulty, and hours, none of which support the claim."}},{"id":"sat_rw_59","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"Expression of Ideas","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"When it was published in 1962, the novel received lukewarm reviews, and its first printing sold poorly. ______, the book slowly found devoted readers, and today it is considered a classic.","question":"Which transition word or phrase best completes the sentence?","choices":{"A":"Similarly","B":"Consequently","C":"Nevertheless","D":"In particular"},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The book's eventual success runs counter to its poor reception, so a concessive transition, 'Nevertheless,' is needed. The later fame was not a consequence of the bad reviews, nor a similar case or a specific example."}},{"id":"sat_rw_60","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"Expression of Ideas","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"Some common insects are surprisingly valuable to farmers. ______, a single ladybug can eat hundreds of aphids in its lifetime, protecting crops without any chemical sprays.","question":"Which transition word or phrase best completes the sentence?","choices":{"A":"On the other hand","B":"Nevertheless","C":"Otherwise","D":"For instance"},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"The ladybug is offered as one specific example of the general claim that some insects help farmers, so 'For instance' fits. The other transitions signal contrast or consequence, which do not match."}},{"id":"sat_rw_61","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"Expression of Ideas","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"(1) Home composting turns kitchen scraps into rich soil for gardens. (2) To begin, choose a shaded spot and add a mix of vegetable peels, leaves, and shredded paper. (3) Within a few months, the pile breaks down into dark, crumbly compost. (4) Gardeners can then spread the finished compost around their plants.","question":"The writer wants to add the sentence \"Turn the pile every week or two so that air can reach the materials inside.\" The sentence would best be placed","choices":{"A":"after sentence 2.","B":"after sentence 1.","C":"after sentence 3.","D":"after sentence 4."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Turning the pile is part of maintaining it while it breaks down, so the sentence belongs right after the setup instructions in sentence 2 and before the finished compost appears in sentence 3."}},{"id":"sat_rw_62","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"Expression of Ideas","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"A student is writing about giant sequoia trees and takes the following notes: Some sequoias are more than 3,000 years old; a sequoia's bark can be two feet thick; the trees grow only on the western slopes of California's Sierra Nevada; a mature sequoia can stand taller than a 25-story building; sequoia cones can remain closed for years until the heat of a fire opens them.","question":"The student wants to emphasize how ancient the oldest sequoias are. Which choice best accomplishes that goal?","choices":{"A":"A sequoia's bark, which can be two feet thick, protects the tree from insects and fire.","B":"Some sequoias were already a thousand years old when the Roman Empire fell.","C":"Sequoias grow only on the western slopes of the Sierra Nevada in California.","D":"A mature sequoia can stand taller than a 25-story building."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Choice B dramatizes the trees' age by anchoring it to a famous historical milestone, which emphasizes antiquity. The other choices, drawn from the notes, concern bark, location, and height instead."}},{"id":"sat_rw_63","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"Expression of Ideas","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"The city launched its bike-share program with 200 bicycles at 20 stations. Ridership grew steadily, and the city added stations near schools and office parks. Riders say the program has made short trips across downtown faster and cheaper than driving.","question":"Which sentence best concludes the paragraph?","choices":{"A":"Bicycles were invented more than two hundred years ago.","B":"Some residents still prefer to drive their own cars downtown.","C":"With ridership still climbing, the city now plans to double the size of the program next year.","D":"The bicycles in the program are painted bright green."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"A good conclusion follows from the paragraph's story of growth, and choice C does exactly that by reporting the planned expansion. Bicycle history, holdout drivers, and paint color are irrelevant or undercut the point."}},{"id":"sat_rw_64","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"Expression of Ideas","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"A paragraph argues that replacing old streetlights with LED lamps was a wise decision for the town of Fairview. The writer wants to add a sentence that supports this claim.","question":"Which sentence best supports the claim that switching to LED streetlights was a wise decision?","choices":{"A":"LED lamps are available in several different colors.","B":"The town of Fairview was founded in 1887.","C":"Some residents say the new lights look brighter than the old ones.","D":"In the first year after the switch, Fairview's streetlight electricity bill fell by 40 percent."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"A 40 percent drop in the electricity bill is concrete evidence that the decision paid off, directly supporting the claim. Color options, the town's founding date, and a vague opinion about brightness do not show the switch was wise."}},{"id":"sat_rw_65","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"Expression of Ideas","difficulty":"Hard","question":{"paragraph":"______ the museum's new wing cost far more than expected, attendance in its first year broke every record in the museum's history.","question":"Which transition word or phrase best completes the sentence?","choices":{"A":"Although","B":"Therefore","C":"Consequently","D":"In other words"},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The sentence sets up a concession: despite the unexpectedly high cost, attendance soared. 'Although' introduces that concession, while the other options signal cause-and-effect or restatement, which reverse or garble the logic."}},{"id":"sat_rw_66","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"Expression of Ideas","difficulty":"Hard","question":{"paragraph":"A student is writing about the discovery of a planet orbiting a nearby star and takes the following notes: The planet was detected in 2023 by a small university telescope; it orbits closer to its star than Mercury orbits the Sun; despite that closeness, it lies in the zone where liquid water could exist because its star is much cooler than the Sun; earlier surveys had overlooked the star because it was considered too dim to be worth studying.","question":"The student wants to explain why the discovery surprised astronomers. Which choice best accomplishes that goal?","choices":{"A":"The planet was detected in 2023 by a telescope at a small university.","B":"Astronomers were surprised to find a potentially water-bearing planet beside a dim star that earlier surveys had ignored.","C":"The planet's star is much cooler than the Sun.","D":"The planet orbits its star more closely than Mercury orbits the Sun."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Choice B explicitly names the surprise and gives its reason: a possibly water-bearing planet turned up next to a star surveys had dismissed. The other choices state isolated facts from the notes without conveying why astronomers were surprised."}},{"id":"sat_rw_67","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"Expression of Ideas","difficulty":"Hard","question":{"paragraph":"Due to the fact that the chef had a great deal of experience in the preparation of meals in the French style, she was able to prepare the complicated dish in a short period of time.","question":"Which revision of the sentence is most concise and precise?","choices":{"A":"Because of her experience with French cooking, the chef, who was very experienced, prepared the dish quickly and in a short time.","B":"The chef had lots of experience in French-style meal preparation, so she made the dish fast.","C":"Her extensive experience in French cooking allowed the chef to prepare the complex dish quickly.","D":"In a short period of time, the experienced chef's experience in French cooking let her prepare the complicated dish."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Choice C removes the wordy 'due to the fact that' and 'in a short period of time,' keeps precise wording ('extensive,' 'complex,' 'quickly'), and avoids the repetition found in choices A and D and the vagueness of 'lots' and 'fast' in B."}},{"id":"sat_math_29","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Algebra","difficulty":"Easy","question":{"paragraph":"","question":"If 4x + 7 = 31, what is the value of x?","choices":{"A":"6","B":"5","C":"7","D":"8"},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Subtract 7 from both sides to get 4x = 24, then divide both sides by 4 to get x = 6."}},{"id":"sat_math_30","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Algebra","difficulty":"Easy","question":{"paragraph":"","question":"Which of the following values of x is a solution of the inequality x + 9 > 14?","choices":{"A":"3","B":"4","C":"5","D":"6"},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Subtracting 9 from both sides gives x > 5, so the only listed value that satisfies the inequality is 6."}},{"id":"sat_math_31","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Algebra","difficulty":"Easy","question":{"paragraph":"Jada's savings are modeled by the equation y = 250 + 15x, where x is the number of weeks since she opened the account and y is the total amount, in dollars, in the account.","question":"In this model, what does the number 15 represent?","choices":{"A":"The amount deposited each week","B":"The initial amount deposited","C":"The total saved after one year","D":"The number of weeks since the account was opened"},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The number 15 is multiplied by the number of weeks, so it is the amount added each week; 250 is the initial deposit."}},{"id":"sat_math_32","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Algebra","difficulty":"Easy","question":{"paragraph":"A function f is defined by f(x) = x² - 3x + 2 for all real numbers x.","question":"What is the value of f(4)?","choices":{"A":"2","B":"6","C":"10","D":"14"},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Substitute 4 for x: f(4) = 16 - 12 + 2 = 6."}},{"id":"sat_math_33","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Algebra","difficulty":"Easy","question":{"paragraph":"The graph of the equation y = -2x + 9 is a line in the xy-plane.","question":"What is the y-intercept of the graph?","choices":{"A":"(0, 9)","B":"(9, 0)","C":"(0, -2)","D":"(-2, 0)"},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The y-intercept occurs where x = 0. Substituting gives y = 9, so the intercept is the point (0, 9)."}},{"id":"sat_math_34","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Algebra","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"At a theater, adult tickets cost $12 each and child tickets cost $8 each. A group bought 13 tickets in all and paid a total of $124 for the tickets.","question":"How many adult tickets did the group buy?","choices":{"A":"4","B":"6","C":"5","D":"8"},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Let a be the number of adult tickets. Then a + (13 - a) accounts for 13 tickets and 12a + 8(13 - a) = 124 gives 4a + 104 = 124, so a = 5."}},{"id":"sat_math_35","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Algebra","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"","question":"The equation |2x - 3| = 9 has two solutions. What is the sum of the two solutions?","choices":{"A":"-3","B":"3","C":"6","D":"9"},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Either 2x - 3 = 9, giving x = 6, or 2x - 3 = -9, giving x = -3. The sum is 6 + (-3) = 3."}},{"id":"sat_math_36","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Algebra","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"After a 20% discount, a jacket sells for $64.","question":"What was the original price of the jacket, in dollars?","choices":{"A":"72","B":"84","C":"80","D":"100"},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The sale price is 80% of the original price, so 0.8p = 64, which gives p = 80."}},{"id":"sat_math_37","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Algebra","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"Line l in the xy-plane has equation y = 3x - 4.","question":"Which of the following points lies on line l?","choices":{"A":"(1, 1)","B":"(3, 4)","C":"(0, 4)","D":"(2, 2)"},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Substituting x = 2 gives y = 3(2) - 4 = 2, so (2, 2) is on the line; the other points do not satisfy the equation."}},{"id":"sat_math_38","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Algebra","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"","question":"Which inequality is equivalent to -3x + 6 ≤ 21?","choices":{"A":"x ≤ -5","B":"x ≤ 5","C":"x ≥ 5","D":"x ≥ -5"},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Subtract 6 to get -3x ≤ 15. Dividing by -3 reverses the inequality sign, giving x ≥ -5."}},{"id":"sat_math_39","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Algebra","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"A table of values for a function f shows that when x = 1, f(x) = 3; when x = 2, f(x) = 7; when x = 3, f(x) = 11; and when x = 4, f(x) = 15.","question":"Which equation could define f?","choices":{"A":"f(x) = 3x","B":"f(x) = 4x - 1","C":"f(x) = 4x + 1","D":"f(x) = x + 2"},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The outputs increase by 4 for each increase of 1 in x, so the slope is 4. Checking x = 1: 4(1) - 1 = 3, which matches the table."}},{"id":"sat_math_40","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Algebra","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"","question":"The graph of the line y = mx + 5, where m is a constant, passes through the point (3, -1). What is the value of m?","choices":{"A":"-3","B":"-2","C":"-1/2","D":"2"},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Substitute the point into the equation: -1 = 3m + 5, so 3m = -6 and m = -2."}},{"id":"sat_math_41","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Algebra","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"A motorboat travels 36 miles downstream in 2 hours. On the return trip, traveling upstream against the same current, the boat takes 3 hours to travel the same 36 miles.","question":"What is the speed, in miles per hour, of the boat in still water?","choices":{"A":"12","B":"14","C":"15","D":"18"},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The downstream speed is 36/2 = 18 mph and the upstream speed is 36/3 = 12 mph. The still-water speed is the average, (18 + 12)/2 = 15 mph."}},{"id":"sat_math_42","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Algebra","difficulty":"Hard","question":{"paragraph":"The system of equations 3x + ky = 12 and 6x + 14y = 24, where k is a constant, has infinitely many solutions.","question":"What is the value of k?","choices":{"A":"7","B":"2","C":"6","D":"14"},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Infinitely many solutions means the equations are multiples of each other. Dividing the second equation by 2 gives 3x + 7y = 12, so k = 7."}},{"id":"sat_math_43","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Algebra","difficulty":"Hard","question":{"paragraph":"","question":"Which of the following describes all solutions of the inequality |x - 4| < 3?","choices":{"A":"x < 1 or x > 7","B":"-7 < x < -1","C":"x > 7","D":"1 < x < 7"},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"The inequality |x - 4| < 3 means -3 < x - 4 < 3. Adding 4 throughout gives 1 < x < 7."}},{"id":"sat_math_44","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Algebra","difficulty":"Hard","question":{"paragraph":"Functions f and g are defined by f(x) = 3x - 5 and g(x) = x² for all real numbers x.","question":"What is the value of f(g(2))?","choices":{"A":"7","B":"1","C":"13","D":"31"},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"First, g(2) = 2² = 4. Then f(4) = 3(4) - 5 = 7."}},{"id":"sat_math_45","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Algebra","difficulty":"Hard","question":{"paragraph":"","question":"Which of the following points (x, y) satisfies both inequalities y > 2x - 1 and y ≤ -x + 5?","choices":{"A":"(0, 0)","B":"(2, 5)","C":"(4, 2)","D":"(1, 0)"},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"For (0, 0): 0 > -1 and 0 ≤ 5, so both hold. Each other point fails at least one inequality: (2, 5) fails the second, (4, 2) fails the first, and (1, 0) fails the first."}},{"id":"sat_math_46","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Advanced Math & Data","difficulty":"Easy","question":{"paragraph":"","question":"What is the value of √144 + √25?","choices":{"A":"13","B":"19","C":"17","D":"169"},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"√144 = 12 and √25 = 5, so the sum is 12 + 5 = 17."}},{"id":"sat_math_47","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Advanced Math & Data","difficulty":"Easy","question":{"paragraph":"The price of a shirt increased from $40 to $50.","question":"By what percent did the price of the shirt increase?","choices":{"A":"10%","B":"20%","C":"25%","D":"30%"},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The increase is $10, and percent increase is measured against the original price: 10/40 = 0.25, or 25%."}},{"id":"sat_math_48","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Advanced Math & Data","difficulty":"Easy","question":{"paragraph":"A rope is 3.5 meters long. There are 100 centimeters in 1 meter.","question":"What is the length of the rope, in centimeters?","choices":{"A":"350","B":"35","C":"3,500","D":"0.35"},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Multiply by the conversion factor: 3.5 × 100 = 350 centimeters."}},{"id":"sat_math_49","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Advanced Math & Data","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"The solutions of the equation x² - 5x + 6 = 0 are r and s, where r < s.","question":"What is the value of r · s?","choices":{"A":"-6","B":"5","C":"6","D":"11"},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Factoring gives (x - 2)(x - 3) = 0, so the solutions are 2 and 3, whose product is 6."}},{"id":"sat_math_50","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Advanced Math & Data","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"","question":"What is the value of (2⁵ · 2⁻²) ÷ 2²?","choices":{"A":"4","B":"8","C":"32","D":"2"},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Add and subtract exponents for the same base: 5 + (-2) - 2 = 1, so the value is 2¹ = 2."}},{"id":"sat_math_51","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Advanced Math & Data","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"","question":"Which expression is equivalent to (4x² - 3x + 5) - (x² + 2x - 1)?","choices":{"A":"3x² - 5x + 6","B":"3x² - x + 4","C":"5x² - 5x + 6","D":"3x² - 5x + 4"},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Subtract term by term: 4x² - x² = 3x², -3x - 2x = -5x, and 5 - (-1) = 6."}},{"id":"sat_math_52","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Advanced Math & Data","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"A survey asked 200 students whether they ride the bus to school. Of the 120 sophomores surveyed, 90 ride the bus.","question":"What fraction of the sophomores surveyed ride the bus?","choices":{"A":"1/2","B":"2/3","C":"3/4","D":"9/10"},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The fraction is 90/120, which simplifies to 3/4."}},{"id":"sat_math_53","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Advanced Math & Data","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"A scatterplot shows the number of hours students studied for a test and their test scores. The line of best fit for the data is modeled by the equation y = 6x + 55, where x is the number of hours studied and y is the predicted test score.","question":"According to the model, what is the predicted test score for a student who studied 5 hours?","choices":{"A":"75","B":"80","C":"85","D":"90"},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Substitute x = 5 into the model: y = 6(5) + 55 = 85."}},{"id":"sat_math_54","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Advanced Math & Data","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"The mean (average) of five numbers is 14. Four of the numbers are 10, 12, 16, and 18.","question":"What is the fifth number?","choices":{"A":"12","B":"13","C":"15","D":"14"},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Five numbers with mean 14 sum to 5 × 14 = 70. The four known numbers sum to 56, so the fifth number is 70 - 56 = 14."}},{"id":"sat_math_55","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Advanced Math & Data","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"A laptop that originally cost $850 is on sale for $680.","question":"What is the percent discount on the laptop?","choices":{"A":"15%","B":"20%","C":"25%","D":"30%"},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The discount is 850 - 680 = 170 dollars, and 170/850 = 0.20, or 20% off the original price."}},{"id":"sat_math_56","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Advanced Math & Data","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"On a map, 2 centimeters represents an actual distance of 15 kilometers.","question":"If two cities are 12 centimeters apart on the map, what is the actual distance, in kilometers, between them?","choices":{"A":"60","B":"75","C":"105","D":"90"},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"12 centimeters is 6 groups of 2 centimeters, so the actual distance is 6 × 15 = 90 kilometers."}},{"id":"sat_math_57","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Advanced Math & Data","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"","question":"For x ≠ 3, the expression (x² - 9)/(x - 3) is equivalent to which of the following?","choices":{"A":"x + 3","B":"x - 3","C":"x² + 3","D":"3"},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Factor the numerator as a difference of squares: x² - 9 = (x - 3)(x + 3). Canceling the factor (x - 3) leaves x + 3."}},{"id":"sat_math_58","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Advanced Math & Data","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"A car purchased for $24,000 loses 10% of its value each year.","question":"What is the value of the car, in dollars, after 2 years?","choices":{"A":"19,200","B":"20,000","C":"21,600","D":"19,440"},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Each year the car keeps 90% of its value, so after 2 years it is worth 24,000 × 0.9² = 24,000 × 0.81 = 19,440 dollars."}},{"id":"sat_math_59","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Advanced Math & Data","difficulty":"Hard","question":{"paragraph":"If x = 3 is a solution of the equation x² - kx + 12 = 0, where k is a constant,","question":"what is the value of k?","choices":{"A":"5","B":"6","C":"9","D":"7"},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Substitute x = 3: 9 - 3k + 12 = 0, so 3k = 21 and k = 7."}},{"id":"sat_math_60","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Advanced Math & Data","difficulty":"Hard","question":{"paragraph":"In the xy-plane, the line y = x + 2 intersects the parabola y = x² - 4x + 6 at two points.","question":"What is the greater x-coordinate of the two intersection points?","choices":{"A":"4","B":"1","C":"2","D":"5"},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Setting the equations equal gives x² - 5x + 4 = 0, which factors as (x - 1)(x - 4) = 0. The x-coordinates are 1 and 4, and the greater is 4."}},{"id":"sat_math_61","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Advanced Math & Data","difficulty":"Hard","question":{"paragraph":"","question":"If 9^x = 27, what is the value of x?","choices":{"A":"1","B":"2","C":"3/2","D":"3"},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Rewrite both sides as powers of 3: 9^x = 3^(2x) and 27 = 3³. So 2x = 3, giving x = 3/2."}},{"id":"sat_math_62","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Advanced Math & Data","difficulty":"Hard","question":{"paragraph":"The function f is defined by f(x) = -2(x - 3)² + 11 for all real numbers x.","question":"What is the maximum value of f(x)?","choices":{"A":"3","B":"5","C":"9","D":"11"},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"The squared term (x - 3)² is always greater than or equal to 0, so -2(x - 3)² is at most 0. The maximum occurs at x = 3, where f(3) = 11."}},{"id":"sat_math_63","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Geometry & Trig","difficulty":"Easy","question":{"paragraph":"A rectangle has a length of 9 centimeters and a width of 4 centimeters.","question":"What is the perimeter, in centimeters, of the rectangle?","choices":{"A":"13","B":"26","C":"36","D":"52"},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The perimeter is 2(9 + 4) = 2(13) = 26 centimeters. (36 is the area, not the perimeter.)"}},{"id":"sat_math_64","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Geometry & Trig","difficulty":"Easy","question":{"paragraph":"A triangle has a base of 10 inches and a height of 6 inches.","question":"What is the area, in square inches, of the triangle?","choices":{"A":"16","B":"36","C":"60","D":"30"},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"The area of a triangle is (1/2)(base)(height) = (1/2)(10)(6) = 30 square inches."}},{"id":"sat_math_65","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Geometry & Trig","difficulty":"Easy","question":{"paragraph":"Two angles are complementary, meaning their measures add up to 90 degrees. One of the angles measures 28 degrees.","question":"What is the measure, in degrees, of the other angle?","choices":{"A":"52","B":"62","C":"152","D":"332"},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Complementary angles sum to 90 degrees, so the other angle measures 90 - 28 = 62 degrees."}},{"id":"sat_math_66","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Geometry & Trig","difficulty":"Easy","question":{"paragraph":"The perimeter of a square is 36 units.","question":"What is the area, in square units, of the square?","choices":{"A":"36","B":"81","C":"72","D":"144"},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Each side of the square has length 36 ÷ 4 = 9 units, so the area is 9² = 81 square units."}},{"id":"sat_math_67","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Geometry & Trig","difficulty":"Easy","question":{"paragraph":"A rectangular box has a length of 5 feet, a width of 3 feet, and a height of 2 feet.","question":"What is the volume, in cubic feet, of the box?","choices":{"A":"25","B":"30","C":"31","D":"60"},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The volume is length × width × height = 5 × 3 × 2 = 30 cubic feet."}},{"id":"sat_math_68","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Geometry & Trig","difficulty":"Easy","question":{"paragraph":"The diameter of a circle is 18 units.","question":"What is the radius, in units, of the circle?","choices":{"A":"9","B":"6","C":"18","D":"36"},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The radius is half the diameter: 18 ÷ 2 = 9 units."}},{"id":"sat_math_69","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Geometry & Trig","difficulty":"Easy","question":{"paragraph":"An isosceles triangle has a vertex angle that measures 40 degrees. The two base angles of the triangle have equal measure.","question":"What is the measure, in degrees, of each base angle?","choices":{"A":"40","B":"60","C":"70","D":"140"},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The base angles sum to 180 - 40 = 140 degrees. Since they are equal, each measures 140 ÷ 2 = 70 degrees."}},{"id":"sat_math_70","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Geometry & Trig","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"Triangle ABC is similar to triangle DEF, with vertices A, B, and C corresponding to vertices D, E, and F, respectively. The length of side AB is 6, the length of side DE is 9, and the length of side BC is 8.","question":"What is the length of side EF?","choices":{"A":"12","B":"10","C":"11","D":"16"},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The scale factor from triangle ABC to triangle DEF is 9/6 = 3/2. Side EF corresponds to BC, so EF = 8 × (3/2) = 12."}},{"id":"sat_math_71","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Geometry & Trig","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"A circle in the xy-plane has center (2, -3) and radius 5.","question":"Which equation represents the circle?","choices":{"A":"(x + 2)² + (y - 3)² = 25","B":"(x - 2)² + (y + 3)² = 25","C":"(x - 2)² + (y + 3)² = 5","D":"(x + 2)² + (y + 3)² = 25"},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"A circle with center (h, k) and radius r has equation (x - h)² + (y - k)² = r². With h = 2, k = -3, and r = 5, this is (x - 2)² + (y + 3)² = 25."}},{"id":"sat_math_72","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Geometry & Trig","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"A circle has a radius of 10 units. A central angle in the circle has a measure of 36 degrees.","question":"What is the length of the arc cut off by this angle?","choices":{"A":"π","B":"4π","C":"2π","D":"20π"},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The arc is 36/360 = 1/10 of the circumference. The circumference is 2π(10) = 20π, so the arc length is (1/10)(20π) = 2π."}},{"id":"sat_math_73","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Geometry & Trig","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"A segment in the xy-plane has endpoints (2, 6) and (8, -2).","question":"What are the coordinates of the midpoint of the segment?","choices":{"A":"(3, 4)","B":"(5, 2)","C":"(6, 8)","D":"(10, 4)"},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The midpoint is the average of the endpoints: ((2 + 8)/2, (6 + (-2))/2) = (5, 2)."}},{"id":"sat_math_74","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Geometry & Trig","difficulty":"Medium","question":{"paragraph":"In a right triangle, an acute angle measures 30 degrees and the hypotenuse has length 20 units.","question":"What is the length, in units, of the side opposite the 30-degree angle?","choices":{"A":"8","B":"10","C":"17","D":"40"},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"By the sine ratio, sin(30°) = opposite/20. Since sin(30°) = 1/2, the opposite side has length 20 × (1/2) = 10 units."}},{"id":"sat_math_75","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Geometry & Trig","difficulty":"Hard","question":{"paragraph":"","question":"If sin(2x + 10) = cos(3x + 5), where (2x + 10) and (3x + 5) are the measures, in degrees, of two complementary angles, what is the value of x?","choices":{"A":"10","B":"12","C":"15","D":"18"},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"For complementary angles, the sine of one equals the cosine of the other, so the angle measures sum to 90: (2x + 10) + (3x + 5) = 90. Thus 5x = 75 and x = 15."}},{"id":"sat_math_76","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Geometry & Trig","difficulty":"Hard","question":{"paragraph":"A 41-foot ladder leans against a vertical wall. The base of the ladder is 9 feet from the base of the wall, and the top of the ladder rests against the wall.","question":"How many feet above the ground does the top of the ladder reach?","choices":{"A":"40","B":"32","C":"38","D":"50"},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"By the Pythagorean theorem, the height reached is √(41² - 9²) = √(1681 - 81) = √1600 = 40 feet."}},{"id":"sat_math_77","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Geometry & Trig","difficulty":"Hard","question":{"paragraph":"A cone has a radius of 3 units and a height of 4 units. The volume V of a cone is given by V = (1/3)πr²h, where r is the radius and h is the height.","question":"What is the volume, in cubic units, of the cone?","choices":{"A":"24π","B":"36π","C":"48π","D":"12π"},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Substitute r = 3 and h = 4: V = (1/3)π(3²)(4) = (1/3)π(36) = 12π cubic units."}},{"id":"sat_math_78","targetProfile":"teen","domain":"SAT Math — Geometry & Trig","difficulty":"Hard","question":{"paragraph":"In right triangle ABC, the right angle is at vertex C. The hypotenuse AB has length 25, and side AC has length 7.","question":"What is the value of sin(B)?","choices":{"A":"24/25","B":"7/25","C":"7/24","D":"25/7"},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Angle B is at vertex B, and the side opposite it is AC, with length 7. So sin(B) = opposite/hypotenuse = 7/25."}}];